.TH "tpu.yaml" "5" "Slurm Configuration File" "September 2026" "Slurm Configuration File"

.SH "NAME"
tpu.yaml \- Slurm configuration file for TPU accelerator families.

.SH "DESCRIPTION"
\fBtpu.yaml\fR describes the TPU accelerator families present on a cluster.
It is read by the \fBjob_submit/tpu\fR plugin, which validates \fB\-\-layout\fR
requests against it, and by the \fBgres/tpu\fR plugin, which uses it to detect
accelerator chips on a compute node.
.LP
The file is located in the same directory as \fBslurm.conf\fR. It is only
required on clusters that enable one of those plugins.
.LP
The file is parsed as YAML. Changes take effect on \fBscontrol reconfigure\fR
or daemon restart.

.SH "PARAMETERS"
The file contains a single top level key.

.TP
\fBtpu_types\fR
A list of accelerator family descriptions. Each entry accepts the following
keys.
.RS
.TP
\fBtype\fR
The canonical family name, for example \fBtpu7x\fR or \fBv6e\fR. This is the
name users give to \fB\-\-layout\fR. Names are matched exactly; there are no
aliases or abbreviations. Required.
.IP

.TP
\fBshape_per_node\fR
The chip geometry attached to a single node, as an \fIx\fRx\fIy\fR or
\fIx\fRx\fIy\fRx\fIz\fR string, for example \fB"2x2x1"\fR. The number of
components determines the fabric's dimensionality, and therefore how many
components a \fB\-\-layout\fR geometry for this family must have. Required.
.IP
A requested geometry must be a whole multiple of this shape along every axis,
not merely a multiple of its chip count. On a family whose nodes are
\fB"2x2x1"\fR, a request of \fI1x2x4\fR is eight chips (two nodes), but its
first axis is not a multiple of 2, so it is rejected.
.IP

.TP
\fBshape_per_segment\fR
The chip geometry of one segment (the scale beyond which an optical circuit
switch is used), for example \fB"4x4x4"\fR. Must be a whole multiple of
\fBshape_per_node\fR along every axis and have the same number of components.
Required.
.IP
Requests that fit within one segment must divide this shape evenly along
every axis and have their largest dimension at most twice their smallest.
Larger requests are stitched together from whole segments over an
optical circuit switch (OCS), where supported, and so must be a whole multiple
of this shape along every axis.
.IP

.TP
\fBnodes_per_pod\fR
The pod limit: the largest allocation this family can serve. A request larger
than this is rejected at submit time. Must be a whole number of segments.
For a family with no OCS tier this is the size of a single segment.
Required.
.IP

.TP
\fBvendor_id\fR
The PCI vendor ID of these chips, as a hexadecimal string such as
\fB"0x1ae0"\fR. Used by \fBgres/tpu\fR when \fBAutoDetect=tpu\fR is set in
\fBgres.conf\fR. Optional; defaults to \fB"0x1ae0"\fR if omitted.
.IP

.TP
\fBpci_device_id\fR
The PCI device ID of these chips, as a hexadecimal string. Used together with
\fBvendor_id\fR. Optional; autodetection is disabled for the family if
omitted.
.IP
.RE

.SH "NOTES"
The number of chips on a node, the fabric's dimensionality and the number of
nodes in a segment are all derived from \fBshape_per_node\fR and
\fBshape_per_segment\fR, and are not configured separately. A count cannot
express a shape: sixteen chips at four chips per node is four nodes, which
could be \fI2x2x4\fR or \fI1x4x4\fR. Distinguishing the two is the purpose of
\fB\-\-layout\fR.
.LP
Dimensions are never reordered. A multi\-node geometry whose dimensions are not
in non\-decreasing order (for example, \fI4x2x2\fR instead of \fI2x2x4\fR) is
rejected rather than sorted.

.SH "EXAMPLE"
.nf
tpu_types:
  \- type: tpu7x
    shape_per_node: "2x2x1"
    shape_per_segment: "4x4x4"
    nodes_per_pod: 2304
    vendor_id: "0x1ae0"
    pci_device_id: "0x0076"

  \- type: v6e
    shape_per_node: "2x2"
    shape_per_segment: "16x16"
    nodes_per_pod: 64
    vendor_id: "0x1ae0"
    pci_device_id: "0x006f"
.fi

